Q: Calculate the average of several values using a variadic-template function A: <p>I'd tend to suggest that supporting an unconstrained set of types with this is probably not a great idea. A couple of the implementations will allow things like: <code>Mean&lt;bool&gt;(false, true);</code> (yielding <code>false</code>), even though the basic idea of an arithmetic mean of Boolean values doesn't make sense. I think as a minimum, we want to restrict it to taking types for which an arithmetic mean makes sense.</p>
<p>I'm also uncertain (at best) how much sense it makes to directly support multiple types. In reality, you're pretty much stuck with the fact that when you add two numbers together, they're going to be converted to some common type ahead of time, and then the addition done on that common type. I'm not sure how much we really gain by leaving it as individual types, instead of promoting it to the common type up-front.</p>
<p>My immediate reaction would be that most of the time, you'd be better off with an <code>initializer_list&lt;T&gt;</code> than variadic template parameters, and you probably also want to restrict <code>T</code> to a numeric type (what the C++ standard calls an arithmetic, but without <code>bool</code>):</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>template &lt;typename T&gt;
concept Numeric = std::is_arithmetic_v&lt;T&gt; &amp;&amp; !std::same_as&lt;T, bool&gt;;

template&lt;Numeric T&gt;
T mean(std::initializer_list&lt;T&gt; inputs) {
    if (inputs.size()==0) {
        return T{};
    }

    T sum{};
    for (auto const &amp;value : inputs) { 
        sum += value;
    }
    return sum / inputs.size();
}
</code></pre>
<p>My guess is that in this case (passing each item as a parameter, or even as part of an <code>initializer_list</code>) you probably don't plan to use it for enough values that maintaining precision is a big consideration. If that's incorrect, you should be aware that summing floating point numbers well can be somewhat non-trivial in itself. One of the better-known ways of doing that well is with <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/66300370/179910">Kahan Summation</a>.</p>
<p>One other point to consider: the concept I've given above is needlessly restrictive--it only allows a few, fairly specific types. To compute the mean, we really only need a type that supports addition and division. If we care about that, a better definition of our <code>Numeric</code> concept would probably be something along this line:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>template &lt;typename T&gt;
concept Numeric =
    !std::same_as&lt;T, bool&gt;  &amp;&amp;
    requires(T a, T b) {
        { a += b } -&gt; std::convertible_to&lt;T&gt;;
        { a / b } -&gt; std::convertible_to&lt;T&gt;;
    };
</code></pre>
<p>So, if you decide to define your own class for big integers or rational numbers, or something like that, this should allow them as long as they define the relevant operations.</p>
